ng record lows</strong>, close to the 63 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">150 record highs</strong>, far more than the 63 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (65). Mobile's all-time high is 106\u00b0F, on August 26, 2023.","t_chg":"Mobile's nights cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 153 complete years: Mobile's average low has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.15\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.20 to \u22120.10), about \u22122.3\u00b0F since 1873. Mobile's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.23\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.19 to +0.27), about +3.5\u00b0F since 1873. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.47\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.17 to +0.78) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.61\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.31 to +0.90). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 10 months fell. Highs rose in all 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Mobile's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.39 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 67.1 inches a year. The wettest year was 1881 (92.32 in); the driest, 1938 (37.15 in). Mobile almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.2 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 7 daily record highs, 3 daily record lows"}}